\section{Subroutine Entry Point Set (Phase 7.4)}

The set of known subroutine entry points from Phase 6 static analysis.
A subroutine invocation is detected whenever the Z80 program counter
matches one of these addresses, regardless of how control arrived there
(CALL, JP, RST, fall-through).

<<subroutine entry point set>>=
struct SubroutineEntryPoints {
    // Lookup table: entry_points[addr] = true if addr is an entry point.
    // Using a flat array for O(1) lookup at the cost of 16KB.
    std::array<bool, 0x4000> is_entry;
    int count = 0;

    SubroutineEntryPoints() {
        is_entry.fill(false);
    }

    void add(uint16_t addr) {
        if (addr < 0x4000 && !is_entry[addr]) {
            is_entry[addr] = true;
            count++;
        }
    }

    bool check(uint16_t addr) const {
        return addr < 0x4000 && is_entry[addr];
    }
};

// Load entry points from the subroutine_entry_points.txt file.
bool load_entry_points(const char* path, SubroutineEntryPoints& ep);
@

\section{Invocation Record}

Each subroutine invocation is identified by (address, invocation count,
trigger type). Events within the invocation are collected as a vector
(compared as unordered sets).

<<invocation record>>=
enum class TriggerType : uint8_t {
    CONVENTIONAL,   // CALL, JP, RST, fall-through
    INTERRUPT,      // NMI or IRQ fired
};

struct InvocationRecord {
    uint16_t address;           // subroutine entry point
    uint32_t invocation_count;  // Nth call to this address
    TriggerType trigger;
    uint16_t stack_depth;       // SP at entry (proxy for call depth)
    std::vector<EventRecord> events;
};
@

\section{Frame Log}

A [[FrameLog]] collects all invocation records and the final VRAM state
for one frame. Comparison between two implementations operates on this
structure.

<<frame log>>=
struct FrameLog {
    int frame_number;
    std::vector<InvocationRecord> invocations;

    // Final VRAM state at frame boundary for pixel-level comparison.
    std::array<uint8_t, 0x2000> final_vram;

    // Final collision flag at frame boundary.
    uint8_t final_intercept;
};
@

\section{BerzerkMachine Interface (Phase 7.1)}

The abstract interface that both the emulator and any second implementation
must satisfy. Sound is deferred to a later iteration.

<<berzerk machine interface>>=
struct InputState;  // forward declaration from platform.h

class BerzerkMachine {
public:
    virtual ~BerzerkMachine() = default;

    // Lifecycle
    virtual bool init(const char* rom_dir) = 0;
    virtual void reset() = 0;

    // Execution
    virtual void step_frame() = 0;
    virtual void step_instruction() {}  // default no-op; only emulator implements

    // State
    virtual MachineSnapshot snapshot() const = 0;
    virtual void restore(const MachineSnapshot& snap) = 0;

    // Output
    virtual const uint8_t* vram_data() const = 0;

    // Input
    virtual void set_input(const InputState& input) = 0;

    // Event logging (no-op by default; emulator overrides)
    virtual void set_logging_enabled(bool /*enabled*/) {}
    virtual FrameLog take_frame_log() { return {}; }

    // Frame counter
    virtual int frame_count() const = 0;
};
@

